ATTACK BREAKERS
Breaker 1 — Do not place portable administrative recovery secrets inside broadly accessible backups.
Breaker 2 — Customer-specific cryptographic isolation.

A breach of one storage plane should not expose material capable of assisting attacks across every tenant.

Breaker 3 — Hardware-bound administrative authentication where practical.
Breaker 4 — Management-plane IP restrictions.
Breaker 5 — JIT administrative access.
Breaker 6 — Configuration-export monitoring.
Breaker 7 — Credential rotation following any control-plane exposure.
Breaker 8 — Treat firewall backups as Tier-0 security assets.
Breaker 9 — Separate recovery capability from authentication bypass capability.
Breaker 10 — Maintain independent customer-side copies so vendor cloud backup is optional.
THE BOARD-LEVEL QUESTION

Don't ask:

Do we have a firewall?

Ask:

If the company that manages our firewall is breached, what information can the attacker learn about our environment, and can any of that information be used to impersonate an administrator or bypass the controls the firewall is supposed to enforce?

That is the actual risk.

